messages:
kevin (eng-sre): Heads-up — customer X on a private VPC hit our cap today and saw huge tail latency. They asked for a recommended client-side throttling plan. Any consolidated guidance we can send?

maya (eng-infra): Sorry you saw that. We should give a small playbook: soft limits + backoff + circuit breaker + opts for batch/retry. Keep it short.

emily (eng-ml): Agree. Also suggest they prefer concurrency limits over raw qps when using long context models — tokens/sec varies with length.

jon (solutions): Can we include a tiny example they can drop in their edge proxy? Something like headers we emit so their LB can adapt.

ops-bot: alert: private-deploy-customer-123 capacity_exhausted gpu_pool=eu-west-2 free_slots=0 timestamp=2026-06-02T11:12:03Z

maya (eng-infra): Draft suggestion: 1) enforce per-client concurrency cap (default 8 requests) 2) rate limit tokens/sec (default 20k tokens/min) 3) honor 429 + Retry-After header for short-circuiting retries 4) exponential backoff + jitter for retries 5) optional short local queue (~100 items) with oldest-drop under pressure.

kevin (eng-sre): Can we be specific about backoff policy? Customers love concrete numbers.

emily (eng-ml): Suggest: initial_retry=200ms, multiplier=2, max_backoff=10s, jitter=uniform(0,100ms). And total_attempts <= 5. Also, prefer to retry only for idempotent or safe requests — avoid for streaming chat continuations where partial state matters.

jon (solutions): For headers: we can recommend they watch these emitted headers: `X-RW-Capacity-Remaining`, `X-RW-Queue-Length`, `Retry-After`. If we can't add those in Private yet, say: fallback to 429 body with `capacity` field.

sasha (product): Quick note: don't promise headers unless infra can emit them in Private. Maya — are those available today?

maya (eng-infra): In Dedicated/Hosted we already send `Retry-After` and 429. For Private currently we only surface 429 and a brief reason in the body. We can add `X-RW-Capacity-Remaining` in the next private control-plane release but not today. So guidance should include both: prefer headers if present, otherwise parse 429 JSON.

kevin (eng-sre): Ok. Let's craft a one-page customer snippet. I'll paste a minimal example for their edge proxy and a tiny client retry helper.

kevin (eng-sre): ````
# proxy config (pseudo-nginx)
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=rl:10m rate=60r/m; # 1 request per second average
server {
  location /v1/generate {
    limit_req zone=rl burst=20 nodelay;
    proxy_pass http://private-redwood;
    # add a response header our LB can use for metrics
  }
}
````

kevin (eng-sre): And a simple client-side backoff (pseudocode):

kevin (eng-sre): ````
function callWithRetries(req) {
  let delay = 200; // ms
  for (i=0;i<5;i++) {
    resp = http.post(req)
    if (resp.status == 200) return resp
    if (resp.status == 429) {
      let ra = resp.headers['Retry-After'] || resp.json().retry_after || null
      if (ra) wait(msFrom(ra))
      else wait(delay + random(0,100))
      delay = Math.min(delay*2, 10000)
      continue
    }
    // non-retryable error
    throw resp
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries')
}
````

jon (solutions): Nice. Also recommend they track three metrics client-side: success_rate, median_latency, and 429_rate. If 429_rate > 1% over 5m, scale down concurrency by 20% and add 2s penalty before increasing again.

maya (eng-infra): Two more practical notes for private deployments: 1) region capacity ceilings can be much stricter than hosted — call out per-AZ GPU pool exhaustion, 2) burst windows matter — short bursts will be queued locally by the runtime until queue fills and then get 429s; so short, high QPS spikes can be worse than sustained high QPS.

emily (eng-ml): And cost tradeoff: telling customers to reduce concurrency lowers latency variation but raises per-token cost if they keep many parallel sessions. Recommend they test with representative prompts and record tokens/sec distribution.

kevin (eng-sre): Action items: Maya will produce a one-pager (2-3 bullet sections) with the playbook + the sample proxy and client snippets. Jon will add wording for solution teams. Emily will add note about model/context length guidance. Sasha will approve wording for external customer delivery.

maya (eng-infra): I'll draft and drop it into #customer-templates by EOD. Quick sample sentence for the top of the doc: "When running Redwood Private, treat the deployment as capacity-constrained infrastructure: apply client-side admission control, prefer concurrency limits over raw QPS limits for long-context workloads, and implement exponential backoff with jitter on 429s."

jon (solutions): fwiw, for enterprise customers we can offer a small attestation template: if they can surface X-RW-* headers we can give them proactive autoscaling hints.

kevin (eng-sre): Great. Thanks all. If anyone has short changes ping me or add inline in the doc. :thumbsup:
